{"type":"standard","title":"Disjoint union","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Disjoint union</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q842620","titles":{"canonical":"Disjoint_union","normalized":"Disjoint union","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Disjoint union</span></span>"},"pageid":188946,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/PolygonsSetDisjointUnion.svg/330px-PolygonsSetDisjointUnion.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":330,"height":376},"originalimage":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/PolygonsSetDisjointUnion.svg/500px-PolygonsSetDisjointUnion.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":361,"height":411},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1335705780","tid":"dc72e617-fe0e-11f0-86ad-4f8d3313487b","timestamp":"2026-01-30T19:07:03Z","description":"In mathematics, operation on sets","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjoint_union","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjoint_union?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjoint_union?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Disjoint_union"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjoint_union","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Disjoint_union","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjoint_union?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Disjoint_union"}},"extract":"In mathematics, the disjoint union  of the sets A and B is the set formed from the elements of A and B labelled (indexed) with the name of the set from which they come. So, an element belonging to both A and B appears twice in the disjoint union, with two different labels.","extract_html":"<p>In mathematics, the <b>disjoint union</b> <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/30206a16da3623a1244336c395f9f268b69c4aef\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:6.09ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> of the sets <span class=\"texhtml \"><i>A</i></span> and <span class=\"texhtml \"><i>B</i></span> is the set formed from the elements of <span class=\"texhtml \"><i>A</i></span> and <span class=\"texhtml \"><i>B</i></span> labelled (indexed) with the name of the set from which they come. So, an element belonging to both <span class=\"texhtml \"><i>A</i></span> and <span class=\"texhtml \"><i>B</i></span> appears twice in the disjoint union, with two different labels.</p>"}